  "The system that defines the mechanisms for the production, consumption, and distribution of goods, services, and resources is known as the economic system of a country.": "audio/en/0b73ad5a73a16a52.mp3",
  "Public goods are goods and services that are available to all individuals without anyone being excluded — for example, parks, roads, police services, street lights, and basic education.": "audio/en/b77b29e7f7754961.mp3",
  "Economics deals with how individuals and societies make choices to use limited resources to satisfy unlimited wants. Every choice involves an opportunity cost — giving up one option for another.": "audio/en/8b24ffa3473cffd8.mp3",
  "And different systems answer these questions differently: market economies rely on private decisions and demand and supply, planned economies on government control, and mixed economies combine both.": "audio/en/2f4997cee91016b1.mp3",
  "In a mixed economy the market guides profit-making enterprises, innovation and competition, whereas the government plays a role in regulation and provision of public goods. Large public sector companies also play an important role.": "audio/en/132227d7ee9b1b2b.mp3",
  "The answers to the three key questions depend on how resources are organised and who controls the decision-making. The system that defines the mechanisms for production, consumption and distribution of goods, services and resources is the economic system of a country.": "audio/en/85f2f0f742137c14.mp3",
  "In a planned economy a central planning authority makes the major decisions. In a market economy demand and supply decide, with the government acting like a referee. A mixed economy combines both — the market guides enterprise while the government regulates and provides public goods. Most economies today are mixed.": "audio/en/915371a16c8d2167.mp3",
  "A mixed economy combines features of both market and planned economies. The market guides profit-making enterprises, innovation and competition, whereas the government plays a role in regulation and provision of public goods. In a mixed economy we also find large public sector companies. In reality, most economies have features of mixed economic systems.": "audio/en/2dd021296bd00d86.mp3",
  "In an economy, the answers to the three key questions depend on how the resources used to produce goods and services are organised, and who controls the decision-making regarding them. The system that defines the mechanisms for the production, consumption, and distribution of goods, services and resources is known as the economic system of a country. There are three kinds: planned, market, and mixed.": "audio/en/4f900e7fa4a47f0c.mp3",
  "Congratulations! You have completed: Economic Systems and How Choices Are Made. You now know that an economic system defines the mechanisms for production, consumption and distribution; that a planned economy is directed by a central planning authority; that a market economy is guided by demand and supply with the government acting as a referee; that a mixed economy combines both; and that India shifted towards a more market-oriented system after the reforms of 1991.": "audio/en/8fd96e2b180cc112.mp3",
  "Welcome to: Economic Systems and How Choices Are Made. We will look at planned, market and mixed economies, and at how India has changed its own system. Press Begin Topic when you are ready.": "audio/en/bbdd7059edb30bdf.mp3",
  "Different countries answer the three key questions differently. What decides the answer?": "audio/en/957dd9d621f55d7c.mp3",
  "In the first kind, who decides what is produced — and at what price?": "audio/en/8de643d83fc63d5c.mp3",
  "That is: what goods will be produced and how much, how they will be made, who will get to use them, and at what prices.": "audio/en/352227750e7e4f8b.mp3",
  "And they are heavily regulated by the government, through strict permits and licences to produce goods and services.": "audio/en/1a75386369f1fc53.mp3",
  "With limited private ownership, enterprises usually follow the central authority's targets rather than market demand, and are heavily regulated through strict permits and licences.": "audio/en/eba52dc417dc6489.mp3",
  "It ensures safety, law and order — but does not control prices or production.": "audio/en/94c1689097282bb2.mp3",
  "Often the government acts like a referee in a football match — it ensures safety, law and order, but does not control prices or production.": "audio/en/e8f837cbcb4b2a10.mp3",
  "Most real countries are neither purely planned nor purely market. So what kind of economy do they have?": "audio/en/5efdcc750bbf85b8.mp3",
  "The government, on the other hand, plays a role in regulation, consumer protection, the provision of public goods and welfare programmes.": "audio/en/ae0fe36441e08b39.mp3",
  "After independence India followed a more state-led approach similar to a planned economy. By 1991 the country faced serious economic difficulties, and the government introduced major reforms that reduced excessive regulations, encouraged private enterprise, opened the economy to global trade and investment, and increased competition — shifting India towards a more market-oriented system while still retaining an important role for the government.": "audio/en/50205f727645ebe0.mp3",
  "The government played a major role in controlling industries, allocating resources, and regulating production through licences and permits.": "audio/en/152582d9ee4e7cbd.mp3",
  "These reforms gradually shifted India towards a more market-oriented system, while still retaining an important role for the government.": "audio/en/683cd8b1522c10ae.mp3",
  "Every economy faces three key questions — what to produce, how to produce, and for whom to produce — to decide how best to use scarce resources.": "audio/en/eb1cc5d13d8955e5.mp3",
  "The 1991 reforms reduced excessive regulations, encouraged private enterprise, opened the economy to global trade and investment, and increased competition — gradually shifting India towards a more market-oriented system while still retaining an important role for the government.": "audio/en/7daa85e560326fa1.mp3",
  "The government owns most resources and sectors — like land, factories, banks, and transport.": "audio/en/5d053193bc510b08.mp3",
  "Some prominent examples are the US, Japan, and Hong Kong. However, governments play an important role even in these economies.": "audio/en/ce488956d3f92bd9.mp3",
  "In a planned economy, a central planning authority of the government, such as the planning commission, makes all major economic decisions. The government owns most resources and sectors like land, factories, banks and transport. With limited private ownership, enterprises follow the central authority's targets rather than market demand, and are heavily regulated through strict permits and licences. This restricts competition, so there is little motivation to improve quality or innovate.": "audio/en/82d95d4df38e578c.mp3",
  "In a planned economy, a central planning authority of the government, such as the planning commission, makes all major economic decisions.": "audio/en/1c2770b13afa80e4.mp3",
  "In a market economy, the questions of what, how and for whom to produce are addressed mainly by the forces of demand and supply, with little government intervention. The government acts like a referee in a football match — it ensures safety, law and order, but does not control prices or production. Ownership rests largely with individuals and private companies, and competition among many producers offering similar products encourages better quality, lower prices, and innovation.": "audio/en/2628db310c4002f5.mp3",
